{"study_accession":"PRJNA401836","secondary_study_accession":"SRP117051","title":"Integrating resistance functions to predict response to induction chemotherapy in de novo acute myeloid leukemia","description":"This study explored resistance functions and their interactions in de novo AML treated with the \"7 + 3\" induction regimen. We analyzed RNA-sequencing profiles of whole bone marrow samples from 52 de novo AML patients who completed the \"7 + 3\" regimen and stratified patients into CR (n = 35) and non-CR (n = 17) groups. A systematic gene set analysis revealed significant associations between chemoresistance and mTOR (P < .001), myc (P < .001), mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation (P < .001), and stemness (P = .002). These functions were independent with regard to gene contents and activity scores. An integration of these four functions showed a prediction of chemoresistance (area under the receiver operating characteristic curve = 0.815) superior to that of each function alone. Moreover, our proposed seven-gene scoring system significantly correlated with the four-function model (r = .97; P < .001) to predict chemoresistance to the \"7 + 3\" regimen. On multivariate analysis, a seven-gene score of ≥-0.027 (hazard ratio: 11.18; 95% confidence interval: 2.06-60.65; P = .005) was an independent risk factor for induction failure. In summary, Myc, OXPHOS, mTOR, and stemness were responsive for chemoresistance in AML. Treatments other than the \"7 + 3\" regimen need to be considered for de novo AML patients predicted to be refractory to the \"7 + 3\" regimen. Overall design: Patients with AML who completed their “7+3” induction chemotherapy at Taichung Veterans General Hospital (TCVGH) were enrolled. We analyzed bone marrow samples from 52 patients with de novo AML who completed the “7+3” induction chemotherapy. These 52 patients were further referred to as the CR group (n = 35) and non-CR group (n = 17), respectively, according to their treatment response after the “7+3” induction chemotherapy.","pubmed":"31356696","geo_series":"","n_samples":36,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN07602665","study_accession":"PRJNA401836","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"sample","title":"BM226","description":"BM226","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM2771046","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2020-08-24","attributes":[["source_name","CR_bone marrow"],["subject status","acute myeloid leukemia (AML) patient"],["response to standard induction chemotherapy","CR (complete remission)"],["tissue","bone marrow"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR6001811","experiment_accession":"SRX3157173","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"NextSeq 500","read_count":27632442,"base_count":4197646290,"first_public":"2020-08-24","experiment_title":"NextSeq 500 sequencing: GSM2771046: BM226 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN07602666","study_accession":"PRJNA401836","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"sample","title":"BM288","description":"BM288","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM2771069","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2020-08-24","attributes":[["source_name","Non-CR_bone marrow"],["subject status","acute myeloid leukemia (AML) patient"],["response to standard induction chemotherapy","Non-CR (non-complete remission)"],["tissue","bone marrow"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR6001834","experiment_accession":"SRX3157196","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"NextSeq 500","read_count":23983642,"base_count":3643576024,"first_public":"2020-08-24","experiment_title":"NextSeq 500 sequencing: GSM2771069: BM288 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN07602667","study_accession":"PRJNA401836","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"sample","title":"BM286","description":"BM286","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM2771068","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2020-08-24","att